Given plant transfer function is \[G(s)=\frac{1}{s^{2}-1}\] For unity feedback system, \[1+G(s)=0\] \[1+\frac{1}{s^{2}-1}=0\] \[s^{2}=0\] \[s=0,0\] Hence, closed loop poles lie at origin. Therefore, the system is unstable. Now as per the question, we need to cascade a lead compensator so that the closed loop system becomes stable. <img src="img/860e8d706b287d6c.webp" loading="lazy" alt="" style="width:min(100%,288px)"> <strong>Option A:</strong> \[\frac{10(s-1)}{s+2}\] Zero lies in right half plane. Hence, it is a non-minimum phase compensator. Therefore, it is not suitable. <strong>Option B:</strong> \[\frac{10(s+4)}{s+2}\] Pole is closer to origin than zero. Hence, it behaves as a lag compensator. Options C and D are lead compensators. We now check stability using Routh criterion. For <strong>Option C</strong>, \[G_{c}(s)=\frac{10(s+2)}{s+10}\] Open loop transfer function becomes \[G(s)H(s)
=
\frac{10(s+2)}
{(s+10)(s^{2}-1)}\] Characteristic equation: \[1+G(s)H(s)=0\] \[(s+10)(s^{2}-1)+10(s+2)=0\] \[s^{3}+10s^{2}+9s+10=0\] Routh array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^{3} &amp; 1 &amp; 9 \\
s^{2} &amp; 10 &amp; 10 \\
s^{1} &amp; 8 &amp; 0 \\
s^{0} &amp; 10 &amp;
\end{array}\] All elements of first column are positive. Hence, system is stable. For <strong>Option D</strong>, \[G_{c}(s)=\frac{2(s+2)}{s+10}\] Open loop transfer function becomes \[G(s)H(s)
=
\frac{2(s+2)}
{(s+10)(s^{2}-1)}\] Characteristic equation: \[1+G(s)H(s)=0\] \[(s+10)(s^{2}-1)+2(s+2)=0\] \[s^{3}+10s^{2}+s-6=0\] Routh array: \[\begin{array}{c|cc}
s^{3} &amp; 1 &amp; 1 \\
s^{2} &amp; 10 &amp; -6 \\
s^{1} &amp; 1.6 &amp; 0 \\
s^{0} &amp; -6 &amp;
\end{array}\] Sign change occurs in first column. Hence, system is unstable. \[\boxed{\text{(C) } \frac{10(s+2)}{s+10}}\]

A horizontal \(\lambda/2\) dipole is placed at a height of \(\lambda_0/2\) above a perfectly conducting infinite ground plane. Using image theory, the ground plane is replaced by an image dipole. Since the dipole is <strong>parallel</strong> to the conducting plane, the image current is <span class="missing-fig">[figure unavailable]</span> \[I_{\text{image}}=I_0e^{j\pi},\] i.e., the image element has a phase difference of \(\pi\). The array factor is \[\begin{aligned}
    AF &amp;= e^{j\beta\frac{\lambda_0}{2}\cos(90^\circ-\theta)} + e^{j\pi}e^{j\beta\frac{\lambda_0}{2}\cos(90^\circ+\theta)}.
\end{aligned}\] Using \(\cos(90^\circ-\theta)=\sin\theta\), \(\cos(90^\circ+\theta)=-\sin\theta\), and \(\beta=\frac{2\pi}{\lambda_0}\), we obtain \[\begin{aligned}
    AF &amp;= e^{j\pi\sin\theta} - e^{-j\pi\sin\theta} \\
    AF &amp;= 2j\sin(\pi\sin\theta).
\end{aligned}\] The phase term is \[\psi = 2\pi\sin\theta - \pi.\] For maxima, \(\psi = 2n\pi\), which gives \[\begin{aligned}
    2\pi\sin\theta - \pi &amp;= 2n\pi \\
    \sin\theta &amp;= \frac{2n+1}{2}.
\end{aligned}\] For the valid value \(n=0\), \(\sin\theta = 1/2\), which gives \[\theta = 30^\circ, 150^\circ\] The radiation pattern of a half-wave dipole in the \(E\)-plane is \[F(\theta) = \frac{\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\cos\theta\right)}{\sin\theta}.\] The resulting pattern is the product of these two functions. Evaluating the nulls for the combined pattern: \[AF = 0 \implies \pi \cos\theta = n\pi \implies \cos\theta = n \implies \theta = 0^\circ, 180^\circ\] \[\boxed{(B)}\] <div class="sol-block sol-keypoints"><div class="sol-block-title">Key points</div><ul><li><strong>Image Theory:</strong> A horizontal antenna above a ground plane is analyzed by replacing the earth with an image antenna out of phase by \(180^\circ\).</li><li><strong>Pattern Multiplication:</strong> The total radiation pattern of an array is the product of the individual element pattern and the array factor.</li><li><strong>Interference:</strong> The constructive and destructive interference between the direct and reflected waves creates the characteristic multi-lobed radiation pattern.</li></ul></div>
